<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Referenced by package.json build.mac as both `entitlements` and
      `entitlementsInherit`, on top of `hardenedRuntime: true`. Every key here
      switches OFF a hardened-runtime protection, so nothing gets added without
      a reason written down next to it, and a measurement behind it. No double
      hyphen in a comment: XML forbids it, and codesign refuses the whole file
      (__tests__/scripts/entitlements.test.ts).

      Measured on the first signed build (18/11, Electron 44.10, Developer ID,
      hardened runtime; OPERATIONS.md "The first signed build", step 2), each
      variant started on a throwaway HOME: node-pty spawning a shell through its
      spawn-helper and better-sqlite3 opening a database in the main process, the
      vault answering over the local API, the window rendering, and the binary
      run as Node (ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1, as the shared MCP servers and
      tars-slot run it) loading both modules and answering an MCP initialize:

      - no key at all: the binary aborts before any JavaScript runs, "Fatal
        process out of memory: Failed to reserve virtual memory for CodeRange"
        (V8 is refused its JIT region), exit 133;
      - allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory (library validation
        on): everything above passes;
      - allow-jit alone: everything above passes. This is the set kept.
    -->

    <!-- Required by Electron/V8: the JIT writes then executes generated code
         (MAP_JIT). Without it V8 cannot reserve its code range: measured above. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      NOT present: com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory.
      Kept until the first signed build "until measured"; measured then: removed
      together with disable-library-validation, everything above passes. V8 on
      Apple silicon takes MAP_JIT memory, which allow-jit covers.

      NOT present: com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation.
      It was kept because the ad hoc builds had no Team ID to validate against.
      Every Mach-O of the signed app, node-pty's pty.node and spawn-helper and
      better-sqlite3's .node included, is signed by electron-builder with the
      same Developer ID, so library validation lets them load: measured above.
      The check bites: the same app with pty.node and better_sqlite3.node
      re-signed ad hoc fails both requires, "mapping process and mapped file
      (non-platform) have different Team IDs", and opens no window. A native
      module that fails to load is fixed in its signature, never by restoring
      this key.

      NOT present: com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables.

      It was granted here from the file's first commit (4240be1) with no
      justification, and it is not part of electron-builder's own default
      template. Failure it caused: with that entitlement, DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES
      still works against the signed, hardened app, so anyone with plain
      user-level code execution (a malicious npm postinstall in a project the
      user opens, an agent spawned with the dangerously-skip-permissions flag) could
      drop an unsigned dylib and have it loaded into the Tars process, which
      then runs with Tars's bundle identity and whatever TCC grants the user has
      approved for it. Nothing in this app reads or sets any DYLD_* variable
      (grep the tree), so the entitlement bought us nothing and cost us the
      protection. Do not re-add it; if a profiler or sanitizer build ever needs
      DYLD_*, use a separate dev-only entitlements file and never ship it.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
